Z||000002||rockcrawl||09-24-2000||01:22 PM||||I had thought about using the climate controls from a Cadillac for one of my replicas, but it is more work than it's worth. The Fiero has electric servos for the heater controls whereas most other GM cars use vacuum servos. Also you'd need the extra Body control module and all the wiring, and somewhere to mount it all. I have two complete systems from Cadillacs if you want to give it a try.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||09-24-2000||01:40 PM||||I looked into it also, but it looked way to difficult. My Silver 86GT is going to undergo a complete rebuilding this January-Febuary. I wouldnt mind giving that a try, but I wont get around to it until Jan-Feb. How much would you want for the caddy climate control system? If I do decided to do it, I will document the entire process of the install and post it here for others.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-24-2000||02:14 PM||||As some of you are already guessing...... You'll need the entire control system. not just the electronics but the air handler stuff as well.<P>The electronics are likely the easy part. Getting the air duct control motors hooked up is likely to be a real headache. If you need to use anything bigger than Fiero's OE duct motor you could have a real space problem. You can play around with the one behind the radio but the vent one under the duct is a real pain to get to.<P>The other issue is many of these sytems are split systems for driver and passenger. That's a whole diferant ducting system. I don't think there's any practicle way to do one of those. Just the space issue allone....||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||09-24-2000||03:04 PM||||I just figured I might give it a try since the car will be all apart anyway. My experience with the dual zone climate controls are that you can shut off the dual zones, and I would probally just use a single zone system just to avoid the trouble. Those caddy systems you have, are they dual zone systems? Also, Orge, I get the impression you know these systems pretty well, have you given any thought to this system? Got any ideas that might help me? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||09-24-2000||05:04 PM||||i dont care about going digital but i would like to move the control panel maybe to the over head light panel. then maybe i could put in one of those head units that also control an in dash changer.<P>you know that idea just occurred to me when i read this post. except for the tempeture control it seems doable. maybe  put the fan control and buttons in the light and the heat control on an after market choke control or something.  is the temp control a sliding reostat? i haven't realy looked at it but that might make it easier still.<P>sorry...went off on a tangent, but whaddaya think?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||09-24-2000||05:33 PM||||That is possible but it would be a real pain in the butt. If I were you, I would either put a hide-away cd-changer behind the seat, or put an indash changer in the airvents and close them off. Does your car have a sunroof? If not, you could put a changer up there and leave the dash alone.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||09-24-2000||06:10 PM||||Sliding Fiero temp lever is a sliding lever that pushes or pulls on a wire. Not anything electronic about it, simple mechanics in action.<BR>The pushbuttons are electronic, and could be more easily moved.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||09-25-2000||11:21 AM||||hmmmm....||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-25-2000||09:20 PM||||The electrical side of moving the stock control is easy enough, just make the harness allot longer. (Use good soldered joints and shrink wrap them. Some of the coneections can carry a pretty heavy load.)<P>The slider is a choke cable type thing. It's an issue of finding a long enough cable, and making sure the bends aren't so tight it binds.<P>As for the rest...... I used to do allot of heating system work. I was the only person in the shop who A. had the patience for it. B. could fit under most dashes.<P>The car is so small inside I'm not sure a dual zone system would work right even if you could fit it.<P>A single zone system might be posible. You'll have to make extensive mods to the stock ducting to make vacuum mode controls work. (Fiero is still the only car I know of with electric mode doors.) I don't think changing the ductwork is an option unless you are building a whole new dash.<P>I can't help you much more than that w/o seeing the system you wanted to use for a donor. I think overall you're in for an awful lot more trouble than its worth to install digital control.<P>Study the stock systems controls. They are in the haynes book. Once you get the idea of how they work you'll start to see how inviloved changing to vacuum is. For example the motor behind the radio controls 2 doors and has 4 stops.<P>At the least you'd need 2 single diaprham and one double diapragm vacuum motor. You'd also need the controls programmed to work them the right way. On top of midifications to the stock duct and doors to accept the vac motors.<P>You also have the issue of how does the new system control the temp door. That's likely not a simple vac motor on that.<P>Like I said it realy depends allot on the donor system. Ideally you'd find an all electric system that uses electric motors like Fiero but even then you'll need to figure out the temperature door thing. If you find one that uses Fiero style motors it's easier. You may have to rewire something but the electric won't require the duct modifications.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||09-25-2000||10:03 PM||||I am not planing on even trying to build a new dash. I think I will let this idea go this time. Does anyone know of a system that controls electronic doors? so I wouldnt have to convert to vac. I might want to give that a try if I dont have to use vac. doors. Electronics I can do, changing everthing to vac ummm. no. I would still like to do it if anyone can think of a way to do it without converting to vac. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||09-26-2000||11:20 AM||||It would seem to me that the electrical damper motor would be the better way to go. All you would have to do is replace the switch board with an equivalent switch (mabe like a soft touch). The cable operated mechanical damper could be replaced by a servo motor like the ones used in robotics. A rotory dial would contol the amount the servo would operate.<P>Just brain stormin',<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
